/**

@page page_exahype_numerical_scheme Adding a numerical scheme

\tableofcontents


A numerical scheme is technically a generator which takes a solver description and produces a task graph of activities.
Therefore, it is typically implemented through a single method

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def construct_output(self, exahype2_project, peano_project, time_step, add_global_tasks=True):
  ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where `time_step` is the @ref peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph "task graph"
 of the surrounding project's time stepping phase.
If a host couples several solvers, it builds a graph per solver and 
passes `add_global_tasks=False` to all but one of them: Such a scheme leaves the tasks that 
belong to the mesh as a whole (the load balancing switch and the dynamic mesh refinement) 
to the other solver (@ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling). Implementing a numerical scheme
therefore means populating this graph with peano4.api.taskgraph.Task
instances and the dependencies between them.

## Task graph structurex

Below is a simple example for such a task graph - the time step task graph exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler
builds for a Finite Volume solver, as rendered by Graph.visualise():

@image html documentation/exahype2/design/time-step-task-graph.png

The task graph describes what happens during a time step, and in which order. 
It represents the logical activities that have to be completed per time step.
The code below sets this graph up:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
reconstruction_task        = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Reconstruct and apply BCs",    entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
update_cell_task           = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Update cell",                  entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
project_on_faces_task      = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Project on faces",             entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
compute_eigenvalue_task    = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Compute max eigenvalue",        entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
reduce_eigenvalue_task     = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Reduce max eigenvalue",         entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL)
compute_cfl_condition_task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name=f"{self._solver.name}_Compute global CFL condition", entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.GLOBAL_END_OF_STEP)

update_cell_task.add_dependency(reconstruction_task)
compute_eigenvalue_task.add_dependency(update_cell_task)
reduce_eigenvalue_task.add_dependency(compute_eigenvalue_task)
project_on_faces_task.add_dependency(reduce_eigenvalue_task)
compute_cfl_condition_task.add_dependency(reduce_eigenvalue_task)

time_step.add_task(reconstruction_task)
time_step.add_task(update_cell_task)
time_step.add_task(compute_eigenvalue_task)
time_step.add_task(reduce_eigenvalue_task)
time_step.add_task(project_on_faces_task)
time_step.add_task(compute_cfl_condition_task)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The CFL condition is split into three tasks. The update kernel does not
return an eigenvalue. The task "Compute max eigenvalue"
(exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ComputeMaxEigenvalue) invokes a kernel of its own
on each updated patch and stores the maximum eigenvalue in the cell. The task
"Reduce max eigenvalue" (exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ReduceMaxEigenvalue)
takes the maximum over all cells. The task "Compute global CFL condition"
(exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ComputeGlobalCFLCondition) finally derives the
next global time step size from this maximum in unprepareTraversal(). It
touches no cell, i.e. it is a Task.GridEntity.GLOBAL_END_OF_STEP task which
does not need a mesh traversal of its own. The task graph compiler fuses
update, eigenvalue computation and reduction into one mesh traversal. The
same pattern is used by the Runge-Kutta Finite Difference scheme
(exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta), where it follows the
final linear combination. The decision is documented in
src/exahype2/api/fv/adrs/0001-separate-eigenvalue-kernel-and-reduction.md.

Every Task is tagged with the kind of mesh entity it works on
(Task.GridEntity.VERTEX/FACE/CELL) and the tasks it depends on. A new
numerical scheme typically follows the same pattern: create one Task per
algorithmic sub-step, wire up add_dependency() calls to express the required
order, and add every task to the phase's Graph via add_task(). While
developing a scheme, Graph.visualise() renders the resulting graph with
matplotlib to sanity-check the dependency structure.


## Schemes with several stages

A scheme with several stages per time step, such as a Runge-Kutta scheme,
adds one chain of tasks per stage to the same graph. The fourth-order Finite
Differences @ref exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta
are an example: each stage reconstructs its input with a halo, evaluates the
right-hand side, and projects the input of the next stage onto the faces,
from where the next stage's reconstruction reads it. The scheme does not
have to say that each stage needs a mesh traversal of its own. It follows
from the data the tasks use: a task which reads face data another task
writes cannot run in the same traversal, and the task graph compiler hence
starts a new one (@ref peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler.FuseSteps).

Tasks within one graph need unique names, so a scheme which adds the same
kind of task once per stage has to number them. The Runge-Kutta tools which
do not depend on the discretisation in space, i.e. the Butcher tableaus and
the linear combination of the estimates, reside in @ref exahype2.api.rungekutta,
so further Runge-Kutta schemes can reuse them.


## Adding the actual computations

The actual computations are now attached to these tasks: The tasks represent
a logical order, but each then has an @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action set" which does the actual calculations.
While the tasks define the order, the action sets define what is to be computed.


## From hereon

The task graph created by this solver will be taken by the project and will be 
mangled with all the other task graphs of solvers that are tied to the project.
After all, ExaHyPE supports the composition of many solvers.
This concatenated super graph is then handed over to a task graph compiler in
Peano.

Depending on what compiler is chosen, the task graph might be linearised into many
single grid sweeps, where each sweep runs only one task from the graph.
However, there are also compiler variants which quite aggressively parallelise over
a task graph, fuse individual steps, or even map some of the @ref glossary_events_and_action_sets "action sets" into real
tasks in a TBB or OpenMP sense of their own.

How this all works is technically speaking not a properly of ExaHyPE, but of Peano. 
Therefore, all the documentation of these aspects is tied to @ref peano4.api.taskgraph,
while the bigger picture is provided by @ref page_peano4_home.


## Best practices and conventions

- Keep the task graph as atomic as possible. That is, don't create tasks that host
  many action sets. Only group the action sets together that really need to be executed in a single task
  <b>and</b> have a similar access pattern to the mesh. For example, it is not a good
  idea to combine a global (time step or eigenvalue) reduction with a cell update
  task. Keep tasks separate and leave it to the task compiler to optimise things.

*/
